= -2x +5. At x=2, y=1 (covered). At x=3, y=-1, not (3,1). So L2 does not cover (1,2) or (2,2). So fails.\n\nThus this arrangement fails.\n\nMaybe we need to use a different non-sunny line instead of diagonal x+y=5. Could use vertical line x=2 covering (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). Then we have 2 sunny lines left to cover remaining points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(3,1),(3,2). That's 6 points. Could we cover them with two sunny lines? Let's try.\n\nPoints: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(3,1),(3,2). We need two lines.\n\nPotential line L1: slope -1/2 through (1,4),(3,2) slope = (2-4)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. Actually slope -1 is not allowed. So not.\n\nLine L1: slope -2 through (1,4),(3,2) slope = (2-4)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So can't.\n\nLine L1: slope -1/3 through (1,4),(3,2) slope = (2-4)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1 again. So not.\n\nLine L1: slope -1/2 through (1,3),(3,2) slope = (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2, allowed. So L1: y-3 = -1/2 (x-1) => y = -0.5x + 3.5. At x=3, y = -1.5 + 3.5 = 2 => (3,2). So L1 covers (1,3),(3,2). Good.\n\nNow remaining points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,4),(3,1). Need L2 to cover them. Could we find a sunny line covering (1,1),(3,1) horizontal not allowed. (1,1),(1,2) vertical not allowed. (1,1),(1,4) vertical not allowed. (1,2),(3,1) slope = (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2, allowed. So line L2: y-2 = -1/2 (x-1) => y = -0.5x + 2.5. At x=3, y = -1.5 + 2.5 = 1 => (3,1). So L2 covers (1,2),(3,1). Now remaining point: (1,1) and (1,4) not covered. Could we have L2 also cover (1,1)? No, because L2 passes through (1,2). So we need a third line, but we only have two sunny lines. So fails.\n\nMaybe we can use a different non-sunny line: horizontal y=1 covering (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1). But we already used vertical x=2, so we could use horizontal y=1 as non-sunny line, covering (1,1),(2,1),(3,1). Then we have 2 sunny lines left to cover remaining points: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,2),(2,3),(3,2). That's 6 points. Could we cover them with two sunny lines? Let's try.\n\nPoints: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),