t satisfy that? Let's check:\n\n(2,1): 1 = 2-1 => yes. (3,2): 2 = 3-1 => yes. (4,3): 3 = 4-1 => yes, but (4,3) is not in the set because (4,3) has a+b=7 > 6, so not needed. (5,4): not needed. So L1 covers (2,1) and (3,2). Good.\n\n- L2: y = -0.5 x + 3. Let's check points:\n\n(2,2): 2 = -0.5*2 + 3 = -1 + 3 = 2 => yes. (4,1): 1 = -0.5*4 + 3 = -2 + 3 = 1 => yes. (6,0): not needed. So L2 covers (2,2) and (4,1). Good.\n\n- L3: y = -2/3 x + 13/3. Check points:\n\n(2,3): 3 = -2/3*2 + 13/3 = -4/3 + 13/3 = 9/3 = 3 => yes. (5,1): 1 = -2/3*5 + 13/3 = -10/3 + 13/3 = 3/3 = 1 => yes. (8,-? ) not needed. So L3 covers (2,3) and (5,1). Good.\n\n- L4: y = -0.5 x + 2.5. Check points:\n\n(3,1): 1 = -0.5*3 + 2.5 = -1.5 + 2.5 = 1 => yes. (1,2): 2 = -0.5*1 + 2.5 = -0.5 + 2.5 = 2 => yes. So L4 covers (3,1) and (1,2). But (1,2) is already covered by vertical line x=1, but that's okay.\n\nNow we need to check if any points remain uncovered:\n\nList all points:\n\nColumn 1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5). Covered by vertical line x=1.\n\nColumn 2: (2,1) covered by L1; (2,2) covered by L2; (2,3) covered by L3; (2,4) not covered yet. Wait, we missed (2,4). Indeed column 2 has (2,4) because a=2, b ≤ 4. Did we cover (2,4)? Let's check if any line passes through (2,4). L1: y = x - 1 => at x=2, y=1, not 4. L2: y = -0.5*2 + 3 = 2, not 4. L3: y = -2/3*2 + 13/3 = -4/3 + 13/3 = 9/3 = 3, not 4. L4: y = -0.5*2 + 2.5 = -1 + 2.5 = 1.5, not 4. So (2,4) is uncovered. So our configuration fails to cover (2,4). So we need to adjust.\n\nThus the configuration we found does not cover all points. We need to cover all points in column 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4). That's 4 points. We have only 4 sunny lines, each can intersect column 2 at most once. So we need at least 4 distinct sunny lines to cover column 2's 4 points. But we have only 4 sunny lines total, and we also need to cover column 1 points (5 points) and column 3 points (3 points) etc. However we have vertical line x=1 covering column 1. So we need to cover