ach line can also cover at most one point in each of columns 3,4,5.\n\nWe need to cover points:\n\n- Column 3: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3)\n- Column 4: (4,1),(4,2)\n- Column 5: (5,1)\n\nWe need to assign each of these points to some line.\n\nLet's try to assign:\n\n- L1: (2,1) + (4,2) (covers column 4 point (4,2)). Also maybe (3,1) or (3,2) or (5,1) but cannot have more than one per column. So L1 currently covers column 2 point (2,1) and column 4 point (4,2). It could also cover a column 3 point, but we need to check if line through (2,1) and (4,2) also passes through any column 3 point. The line is y = 0.5 x. At x=3, y = 1.5, not integer, so not a required point. At x=5, y = 2.5, not required. So L1 covers only (2,1) and (4,2). Good.\n\n- L2: (2,2) + (4,1) (covers column 4 point (4,1)). The line is y = -0.5 x + 3. At x=3, y = 1.5, not required. At x=5, y = 0.5, not required. So L2 covers (2,2) and (4,1).\n\n- L3: (2,3) + (5,1) (covers column 5 point (5,1)). The line is y = -2/3 x + 13/3. At x=3, y = -2 + 13/3 = ( -6 + 13)/3 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333, not required. At x=4, y = -8/3 + 13/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.667, not required. So L3 covers (2,3) and (5,1).\n\n- L4: (2,4) + (3,1) (covers column 3 point (3,1)). The line is y = -3x + 10? Actually slope = (1-4)/(3-2) = -3, line y = -3x + 10. At x=4, y = -12 + 10 = -2, not required. At x=5, y = -15 + 10 = -5, not required. So L4 covers (2,4) and (3,1).\n\nNow we have covered column 3 point (3,1) via L4. We still need to cover (3,2) and (3,3). We have no lines left. However we could adjust assignments: maybe L1 could also cover (3,2) if we choose a line through (2,1) and (3,2) (slope 1). But then L1 would not cover (4,2). But we could assign (4,2) to another line, maybe L2 or L3 or L4. Let's see.\n\nWe need to cover (3,2) and (3,3). Let's see if we can assign them to lines that already have a column 2 point.\n\n- (3,2) could be paired with (2,1) (slope 1) as before, or with (2,2) (slope 0) not allowed, or with (2,3) (slope -1) not allowed, or with (2,4)