with Q3 = (4,3): slope = (3-4)/(4-3) = -1, not allowed.\n\nPair with R1 = (5,1): slope = (1-4)/(5-3) = -3/2 = -1.5, line y = -1.5 x + something. At x=4, y = -6 + something = 2? Actually compute: line through (3,4) and (5,1): slope = (1-4)/(5-3) = -3/2 = -1.5. Equation: y - 4 = -1.5 (x - 3) => y = -1.5 x + 8.5. At x=4, y = -6 + 8.5 = 2.5, not required. At x=6, y = -9 + 8.5 = -0.5, not required.\n\nPair with R2 = (5,2): slope = (2-4)/(5-3) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n\nPair with S1 = (6,1): slope = (1-4)/(6-3) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus P4 can be paired with Q1 (slope -3) or Q2 (slope -2). Let's keep Q1? But Q1 already used by P3? Actually we assigned Q1 to P3 (L3). But we could assign Q1 to P4 instead, and assign P3 to something else. Let's see.\n\nWe need to cover all points Q1,Q2,Q3,R1,R2,S1. We have 4 lines L1-L4. We have assigned:\n\n- L1: P1 (3,1) with Q2 (4,2) (slope 1). So L1 covers P1 and Q2.\n\n- L2: P2 (3,2) with Q3 (4,3) (slope 1). So L2 covers P2 and Q3.\n\n- L3: P3 (3,3) with Q1 (4,1) (slope -2). So L3 covers P3 and Q1.\n\n- L4: P4 (3,4) currently not assigned to any Q? Could assign to R1 or R2 or S1. Let's see.\n\nNow we have covered Q1, Q2, Q3. Remaining points: R1=(5,1), R2=(5,2), S1=(6,1). We have L4 free (currently only covering P4). L4 can be assigned to one of these points (or more if collinear). Let's see if we can find a line through P4 (3,4) and R1 (5,1). That line has slope -1.5, which is sunny. So L4 could be (3,4)-(5,1). That covers P4 and R1. Then we still need to cover R2 and S1. We have no lines left (we used all 4 lines). But we could assign R2 and S1 to some of the existing lines if they are collinear with the points already on those lines. Let's check:\n\n- L1: line through (3,1) and (4,2) has equation y = x - 2. At x=5, y = 3, not (5,2). At x=6, y = 4, not (6,1). So L1 cannot cover R2 or S1.\n\n- L2: line through (3,2) and (4,3) has equation y = x - 1. At x=5, y = 4, not (5,2). At x=6, y = 5, not (6,1). So L2 cannot cover R2 or 